AWS-002 — Workflow Principles

Document ID: AWS-002
Title: Workflow Principles
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification establishes the fundamental principles governing the design, execution, orchestration, governance, monitoring, and evolution of workflows within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

These principles form the constitutional foundation for every Canonical Workflow Object (CWO).

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Workflow principles
Execution integrity
Workflow quality
Orchestration principles
Participant interaction
Workflow evolution
Execution interoperability

These principles apply to every Atlas workflow.

3. Canonical Definition

A Workflow Principle is a normative statement defining an immutable characteristic that every Atlas workflow SHALL satisfy regardless of implementation technology or engineering domain.

Workflow principles constrain workflow design.

They do not define individual workflows.

4. Objectives

Workflow Principles SHALL:

Preserve engineering intent.
Ensure deterministic execution.
Support explainability.
Enable interoperability.
Preserve governance.
Enable automation.
Support optimization.
Preserve engineering integrity.
5. Foundational Principles

Atlas defines the following foundational workflow principles.

WP-001 — Explicit Definition

Every workflow SHALL be explicitly defined.

Implicit workflows SHALL NOT exist.

WP-002 — Deterministic Execution

Equivalent workflow definitions with equivalent inputs SHALL produce equivalent execution behavior unless adaptive execution has been explicitly authorized.

WP-003 — Explainability

Every workflow execution SHALL explain:

Activities executed
Decisions taken
Transition reasons
Responsible participants
Produced outputs
WP-004 — Traceability

Every workflow SHALL preserve:

Execution history
State transitions
Inputs
Outputs
Participants
Timing
Decisions
Evidence

Execution history SHALL remain immutable.

WP-005 — Technology Independence

Workflow semantics SHALL remain independent of:

Workflow engines
Programming languages
Cloud providers
Messaging systems
Infrastructure
Automation platforms
WP-006 — Separation of Definition and Execution

Workflow Definitions describe execution behavior.

Workflow Executions perform execution.

Definitions SHALL NOT become execution records.

WP-007 — Governance

Every workflow SHALL be governed by applicable Canonical Policy Objects.

No workflow SHALL bypass governance.

WP-008 — Modularity

Workflow activities SHOULD remain independently reusable.

Large workflows SHOULD be composed from smaller workflow modules.

WP-009 — Composability

Independent workflows SHALL support governed composition.

Composition SHALL preserve workflow semantics.

WP-010 — Version Awareness

Every workflow execution SHALL reference the exact workflow version executed.

Historical executions SHALL remain reproducible.

WP-011 — Participant Accountability

Every activity SHALL identify:

Responsible participant
Responsible system
Responsible AI agent (where applicable)

Responsibility SHALL remain attributable.

WP-012 — Context Awareness

Workflow execution MAY depend upon context.

Context SHALL be explicitly defined.

Implicit context SHALL NOT exist.

WP-013 — Recoverability

Every workflow SHALL support governed recovery from execution failures.

Recovery SHALL preserve workflow integrity.

WP-014 — Observability

Workflow execution SHALL expose sufficient operational information for:

Monitoring
Diagnostics
Audit
Performance analysis

Observability SHALL NOT alter workflow behavior.

WP-015 — Continuous Evolution

Workflow definitions MAY evolve.

Evolution SHALL preserve:

Identity
Version lineage
Traceability
Governance
Execution reproducibility
6. Workflow Quality Principles

Every workflow SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Completeness
Determinism
Explainability
Traceability
Recoverability
Maintainability
Scalability
Auditability

Quality SHALL be continuously evaluated.

7. Workflow Execution Principles

Every workflow execution SHALL support:

Initialization
Activity execution
State transitions
Decision processing
Completion
Failure handling
Recovery
Finalization

Execution SHALL remain governed throughout its lifecycle.

8. Workflow Interoperability Principles

Workflow definitions SHALL support:

Cross-platform execution
Cross-organization execution
Federated orchestration
Semantic preservation
Version compatibility

Interoperability SHALL preserve workflow meaning.

9. Workflow Security Principles

Workflow execution SHALL preserve:

Confidentiality
Integrity
Availability
Authenticity
Non-repudiation

Security SHALL remain governed by APS.

10. Workflow Governance Principles

Every workflow SHALL define:

Owner
Steward
Execution authority
Review authority
Audit requirements
Applicable policies

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout the workflow lifecycle.

11. Conformance

A workflow conforms to this specification if it:

Satisfies every foundational principle.
Supports governed execution.
Supports traceability.
Supports explainability.
Supports interoperability.
Supports versioning.
Supports recoverability.
12. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-native execution principles.
Autonomous workflow optimization.
Adaptive orchestration principles.
Distributed workflow reasoning.
Self-healing workflow principles.
Digital Twin execution principles.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical workflow principles.

